  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current method relies on simulated annealing with local SLSQP refinements, but the risk of local optima remains high due to reliance on random perturbations. Here, we propose a diffusion-driven optimization that models quality propagation: \"good\" solutions continuously attract mass transfers while \"bad\" solutions lose mass. The optimization dynamics are governed by the gradient of the maximum correlation, with innovation governed by Poisson-driven resets. This approach leverages physics-inspired flows to escape local optima by:\n\n1. Establishing pairwise utility gradients that measure influence on correlation peaks\n2. Atomizing gradient contributions and applying  Fick's law of diffusion\n3. Hybridizing with gradient descent when local refinement is warranted\n4. Employing stochastic reset events to explore new regions\n\nThe key theoretical improvement is treating the optimization as a physical system in state-space, with solutions as distribution densities evolving via Fokker-Planck dynamics. This provides robust cross-scale exploration and local optimization simultaneously.\n\nMathematical specification:\nRepresentation: A real-valued NumPy array h \u2208 [0,1]^n with exact sum n/2\nObjective: Minimize C5(h) = max_k (\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx) subject to:\n    (i) 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n    (ii) \u2211h_i = n/2\n\nAlgorithm: \n1. Compute projective connectivity graph where elements influence C5 via lagged contributions\n2. Estimate component-wise gradient: dh_i / C5 \u2248 (1 / C5_max) * \u2211k \u03b7_k * (1 - h_{i+k}) * indicator_{k\u2208Support}\n3. Apply diffusion operator: \u0394h_i = -\u2207C5 * h_i (diffusion away from peaks) + \u03c3 * PoissonResetFn(i)\n4. Implement dual-gradient descent with anisotropic diffusion coefficients\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization: \n   - Use parent solution if available via linear scaling\n   - Enforce 0\u2264h\u22641 with projected gradient projection method\n   - Set diffusion coefficient \u03c3 = 0.1 initially\n\n2. Phase One: Diffusion-Driven Evolution\n   For each iteration within budget:\n    - Compute current C5 via: C5 = np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,\"full\"))*(2.0/n)\n    - Estimate gradients for each component through:\n        gradient[i] = sum_{k} clip( correlation_gradient(i,k), -0.1, 0.1 )\n    - Apply diffusion: h[i] += \u03b7 * gradient[i] clipped appropriately\n    - Apply Poisson reset on stochastic subset: \n        for 5% of indices: h[i] = base_value + noise\n    - Enforce constraints: \n        * Projection: h[i] = max(min(h[i],1),0)\n        * Sum adjustment: Gradual transfer between index pairs\n\n3. Phase Two: Adaptive Refinement\n   For each beta schedule:\n    - Use SLSQP smoothing with objectives (C5) and constraints\n    - Increase beta exponentially (50, 200, 800, ...)\n\nSearch choices:\n- Start sigma from 0.1 and increase to 0.5 as progress stalls\n- Reset frequency decays exponentially: f(t) = exp(-t/T) for T=100s\n- Poisson reset injects fresh gaussian noise with mean=0.5, std=0.1\n- Gradient descent activation when |gradient| > 0.5 for any component\n\nCompute budget: \n- GA-style cost evaluation: 70% budget (simulated annealing not used)\n- Local refinement: 30% budget \n- Total evals: budget/time_per_eval\n\nValidation:\n- Final candidate passes 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum=1 clip/adjust\n- Recompute C5 to confirm improvement over current 0.38088\n- Maintain parent fallback if budget exhausted\n\nObjective design:\ndef compute_c5(h):\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n    return np.max(corr) * (2.0 / len(h))\n\nThe optimization mimics Fick's second law while adding Poissonian jumps to counter gradient concentration. This ensures continuous exploration and refinement.",
